# More FTDM cases (added Nov 2026), modeled on the FORMAT of the three past district events the user shared:
#   2026: financial advisors meet a client (new parents) nervous about the future (personal finance + trust)
#   2025: small business advisors help a client start a business (ownership, finance, insurance, budgets)
#   2024: bank managers create materials for customers (savings products, markets, writing)
# Each case: an advisor team, one client (judge), 5 PIs, a "your team will discuss" list, two judge questions,
# and model-answer notes ending with strong answers to both judge questions. Situations, numbers and answers are
# original; real-world facts come from talking.py and the Finance Hub (checked Nov 2026).
CASES = []
def case(id, area, role, judge, situation, tasks, pis, questions, model):
    CASES.append(dict(id=id, area=area, role=role, judge=judge, situation=situation, tasks=tasks, pis=pis,
                      questions=questions, model=model, style="client"))

# ======================= Personal-finance clients (2026 format) =======================
case("single-parent-reset","Financial Analysis","financial advisors","recently divorced single parent of two",
 "A 38-year-old client (judge) finalized a divorce two months ago and now supports two children, ages 6 and 10, on a $64,000 salary plus $900 a month in child support. The client kept the house (a $1,450 monthly mortgage payment), has $4,000 in savings and a 401(k) worth $38,000. The client's ex-spouse is still listed as the beneficiary on the 401(k) and a work life insurance policy. The client has never managed the household budget alone and feels anxious about money for the first time.",
 ["Building a budget on one income","Setting financial goals for the next three years","Insurance needs as the sole provider","Estate planning steps after a divorce","How the advisors will earn the client's trust"],
 ["FI:066","FI:065","FI:571","FI:572","EI:128"],
 ["What happens to my kids financially if something happens to me tomorrow?","Should I sell the house to have more breathing room?"],
 ["Open with empathy and trust (EI:128): 'Lots of families go through this, and you're doing the right thing by planning now.' Explain how we're paid, and that you'll get a written plan with no surprises.",
  "Budget (FI:066): start from net income (salary take-home plus $900 child support), list fixed costs first (mortgage, insurance, childcare), then variable. Use 50/30/20 as a guide, but housing alone may be above 30%, so trim wants first.",
  "Goals (FI:065), SMART: $1,000 starter emergency fund in 60 days; 3 months of expenses (about $12,000) in 18 months; keep the full 401(k) match every paycheck; start a 529 for each child at $50 a month.",
  "Insurance (FI:571): as the only earner, term life of about 10 to 12 times income (20-year term covers both kids to adulthood) and long-term disability insurance replacing about 60% of pay. About 1 in 4 of today's 20-year-olds will become disabled before retirement age (SSA).",
  "Estate (FI:572), urgent: beneficiary designations override a will, so the ex-spouse would still receive the 401(k) and life insurance today. Update every beneficiary form this week, write a will naming a guardian, and set up a trust or custodian so the kids' money is managed until adulthood.",
  "Protect the income: confirm health insurance for the kids, and keep child support in a separate account tracked against the budget.",
  "Automate: transfer savings the day after payday so it happens before spending. Review the plan in six months and at each life change.",
  "Visual to hand the judge: a one-page budget pie (needs / wants / savings) and a 3-year timeline of the goals.",
  "Judge Q1: Today, the 401(k) and work policy would go to your ex-spouse because of the old beneficiary forms, so we fix that first. With updated beneficiaries, a will naming a guardian, a trust for the kids and a term life policy, they'd have money for housing and college managed by someone you choose.",
  "Judge Q2: Not yet. Selling has costs (often 6% to 10% with agent fees and moving) and uproots the kids. Let's run the budget for three months. If housing stays above about 35% of take-home pay and savings can't grow, then downsizing becomes the right move, and we'd plan it carefully."])

case("gig-worker","Financial Analysis","financial planners","full-time rideshare driver and freelance designer",
 "A 27-year-old client (judge) earns money three ways: rideshare driving, freelance graphic design and tips from weekend catering work. Income swings from $2,800 to $6,000 a month and totaled about $52,000 last year. Nothing is withheld for taxes, and the client owed $4,100 in April with a penalty. The client has no retirement account, no health insurance through an employer and $1,800 in savings. The client wants stability without giving up the freedom of gig work.",
 ["Sources of income and how each is taxed","Budgeting with irregular income","Preparing for income taxes","Retirement options for the self-employed","Insurance needs for gig workers"],
 ["FI:061","FI:067","FI:066","FI:569","FI:571"],
 ["How much should I set aside for taxes from each payment?","Is it worth getting a regular part-time job just for the benefits?"],
 ["Open: 'You can keep your freedom and still get a paycheck-like system. We'll build it around your lowest month.'",
  "Income (FI:061): driving and design are self-employment income (1099-NEC/1099-K); catering tips are tip income. Under the 2025 tax law, up to $25,000 of qualified tips is deductible from federal income tax for 2025-2028, but payroll taxes still apply.",
  "Taxes (FI:067): self-employment tax is 15.3% (Social Security + Medicare, both halves) on net earnings, plus income tax. Pay quarterly estimated taxes (April, June, September, January) to avoid penalties. Track mileage and business expenses, which lower taxable profit on Schedule C.",
  "Budget (FI:066): budget from the lowest typical month ($2,800). Every payment goes into one 'income' account; pay yourself a fixed salary from it; extra in good months goes to a buffer that covers slow months.",
  "Account system: (1) income account, (2) tax account, 25% to 30% of every payment moved automatically, (3) bills, (4) buffer and emergency fund.",
  "Retirement (FI:569): a SEP-IRA or Solo 401(k) lets self-employed people save much more than a regular IRA. A Roth IRA ($7,500 limit in 2026 and 2027) is great while income is moderate. Starting in 2027, the Saver's Match can add up to $1,000 a year for eligible savers.",
  "Insurance (FI:571): health insurance through the HealthCare.gov marketplace (a high-deductible plan with an HSA can work), disability coverage, and confirm rideshare insurance, since personal auto policies often exclude driving for an app.",
  "Visual: a flow diagram showing each payment splitting into tax, bills, buffer and savings.",
  "Judge Q1: Move 25% to 30% of every payment into a separate tax account the day it arrives. That covers 15.3% self-employment tax plus income tax at your level. Then pay quarterly estimates from that account so April has no surprises and no penalty.",
  "Judge Q2: Only if the benefits are worth more than the time. Employer health insurance can be worth thousands a year, and a 401(k) match is free money. Compare that against what you'd earn in the same hours driving or designing. If marketplace coverage is affordable for you, the freedom may be worth more."])

case("newlyweds-merge","Financial Analysis","financial advisors","newly married couple combining finances",
 "Newlyweds (judge), both 29, just combined households. One spouse earns $71,000, has a 790 credit score and $22,000 saved. The other earns $56,000, has a 640 credit score after missed payments in college, $14,000 in credit card debt and no savings. They argue about money and want to buy a home within two years. They don't know whether to combine all their accounts or keep them separate.",
 ["Setting shared financial goals","How credit history affects borrowing","Applying for a mortgage","Why saving and investing matter for their future","The advisors' responsibilities to both spouses"],
 ["FI:065","FI:072","FI:625","FI:270","CR:012"],
 ["Should we combine all our money into joint accounts?","Should we apply for the mortgage using only the spouse with the better credit?"],
 ["Responsibilities (CR:012): we work for both of you equally, keep everything confidential and explain every option and its cost.",
  "Goals (FI:065): agree on shared SMART goals first: pay off the $14,000 card in 18 months, build a $15,000 emergency fund, and save a $30,000 down payment in 24 months.",
  "Account structure: 'yours, mine and ours': a joint account for shared bills and goals, funded by each spouse in proportion to income (about 56% / 44%), plus personal spending money for each. It cuts arguments while keeping autonomy.",
  "Credit history (FI:072): pull all three reports free at AnnualCreditReport.com and dispute errors. Payment history is about 35% of a FICO score and utilization about 30%, so on-time autopay and paying the card down below 30% of its limit raise the 640 score fastest.",
  "Mortgage (FI:625): lenders review credit, income (pay stubs, W-2s), debt-to-income ratio and down payment. 20% down avoids private mortgage insurance. Get pre-approved by 2-3 lenders within about two weeks so inquiries count as one.",
  "Save and invest (FI:270): the down payment belongs in a high-yield savings account or CDs (top accounts paid about 4.35% APY in Nov. 2026) because it's needed in two years. Retirement money stays invested in index funds for growth.",
  "Avalanche the 24% card first: at about 22% average card rates, paying it off is a guaranteed return better than any investment.",
  "Visual: a side-by-side timeline: months 1-18 debt payoff, months 1-24 down payment growth, credit score milestones.",
  "Judge Q1: Combine the shared part. A joint account for bills and goals, each contributing by income share, plus a personal account each for guilt-free spending. Couples who agree on a system argue less, and you both see progress toward the house.",
  "Judge Q2: Possibly. Applying on one income with the 790 score may get a better rate, but it limits how much you can borrow. Let's compare both ways at pre-approval. In 12 to 18 months of on-time payments and lower card balances, the 640 score could rise enough to apply jointly at a good rate."])

case("teen-first-job","Financial Analysis","bank personal bankers","parent of a 16-year-old starting a first job",
 "A parent (judge) visits the bank with questions about their 16-year-old, who just got a part-time job at a grocery store earning $14 an hour for about 15 hours a week. The teen has never had a bank account and was confused by the first pay stub. The parent wants the teen to learn good money habits, avoid debt mistakes and start saving for college and a car, but worries about online scams and overspending on apps.",
 ["Reading a pay stub and understanding sources of income","Choosing a financial-services provider and account","Managing online accounts safely","The need to save and invest","The purposes and risks of credit"],
 ["FI:068","FI:076","FI:830","FI:270","FI:002"],
 ["Should my teen get a credit card now to start building credit?","How can my teen save for both a car and college on a small paycheck?"],
 ["Open: 'Learning this at 16 is a huge head start. Habits built now last a lifetime.'",
  "Pay stub (FI:068): gross pay ($14 x 15 = $210 a week), then deductions: federal and state income tax withholding (often small or none at this income, set by the W-4), Social Security 6.2% and Medicare 1.45% (FICA). Net pay is what lands in the account. Year-to-date totals help track earnings.",
  "Taxes: a teen with withholding should still file a return, because tax withheld may be fully refunded if income is below the standard deduction ($16,100 single for 2026).",
  "Choosing a provider (FI:076): compare fees, minimums, FDIC (bank) or NCUA (credit union) insurance up to $250,000, mobile app quality and parental controls. A teen checking account linked to a parent's account with no monthly fee fits best.",
  "Online safety (FI:830): strong unique password, app-based two-factor login, transaction alerts, never share codes. Under Regulation E, reporting an unauthorized debit within 2 business days caps losses at $50.",
  "Save and invest (FI:270): pay yourself first: split direct deposit so 30% goes straight to savings. A custodial Roth IRA is possible with earned income, and money invested at 16 has 50 years to compound.",
  "Credit (FI:002): credit lets you buy now and pay later at a cost. Average card rates were about 22% in 2026. Building credit can start safely as an authorized user on a parent's card, with no debt risk to the teen.",
  "Visual: a pie chart of one paycheck: spend / car fund / college fund / Roth IRA.",
  "Judge Q1: Not on their own yet. Under-21 card applicants need independent income or a cosigner, and mistakes at 22% interest are expensive. Adding your teen as an authorized user on your card, with a small spending limit, builds their credit history safely, and you can watch the habits.",
  "Judge Q2: Split every paycheck automatically into separate buckets, for example 20% car fund and 10% college or Roth IRA, both in a high-yield savings account. At about $190 a week net, that's roughly $57 a week saved, nearly $3,000 a year, plus interest."])

case("market-drop-retiree","Financial Analysis","financial advisors","63-year-old client panicking after a market drop",
 "A 63-year-old client (judge) plans to retire in two years with $610,000 in a 401(k) that is 85% stocks. After a recent 14% market drop, the client called wanting to move everything to cash. The client expects about $2,600 a month from Social Security at full retirement age (67) and needs about $5,200 a month to live. The client remembers 2008 and is losing sleep.",
 ["How economic conditions affect financial markets","Planning retirement income","Types of investments for someone near retirement","Responding to the client's emotions with empathy","The advisor's responsibilities in a crisis"],
 ["FI:574","FI:569","FI:077","EI:030","CR:012"],
 ["What if the market keeps falling right after I retire?","When should I start taking Social Security?"],
 ["Empathy first (EI:030): 'Losing sleep over this is completely understandable, especially after 2008. Let's look at what this drop actually means for your plan.'",
  "Responsibilities (CR:012): we're acting in your best interest, which sometimes means advising against what feels urgent. Selling now locks in the loss.",
  "Markets and the economy (FI:574): markets fall and recover with the business cycle. The S&P 500 fell about 37% in 2008 and 18% in 2022 and recovered both times. With GDP growing 3.4% last quarter and unemployment at 4.1%, the economy is still expanding.",
  "The real issue: 85% stocks is too aggressive two years from retirement. Shift gradually toward about 50-60% stocks, with bonds and cash, rather than all at once in a panic.",
  "Income plan (FI:569): need $5,200 a month; Social Security covers $2,600; the gap is $2,600 a month ($31,200 a year). At a 4% withdrawal rate, $610,000 supports about $24,400 a year, so we close the rest by working part-time briefly, delaying Social Security or trimming spending.",
  "Bucket strategy (FI:077): bucket 1, two years of withdrawals in cash and short-term Treasuries; bucket 2, bonds for years 3-7; bucket 3, stock index funds for growth. Market drops never force selling stocks.",
  "Sequence-of-returns risk: a crash early in retirement does the most damage, which is exactly what the cash bucket protects against.",
  "Visual: a three-bucket diagram and a line chart of the S&P 500 recovering after 2008 and 2022.",
  "Judge Q1: That's why we build the cash bucket now. With two years of spending in cash and Treasuries, you won't have to sell stocks in a downturn, giving them time to recover, as they did after 2008 and 2022.",
  "Judge Q2: If you're healthy, waiting usually pays. Claiming at 62 cuts the benefit permanently by up to 30%, while each year after 67 adds 8% until 70. Since your Social Security covers half your needs, delaying even to 67 or 68 makes your guaranteed income bigger for life, and benefits rise with inflation (3.6% in 2027)."])

# ======================= Small-business clients (2025 format) =======================
case("bakery-partners","Entrepreneurship","small business advisors","talented baker starting a business with a friend",
 "A client (judge) has baked custom cakes from home for three years and now wants to open a small bakery with a friend who has restaurant management experience. The client would contribute $25,000 and the friend $15,000. They found a storefront for $3,200 a month and estimate $85,000 in start-up costs for equipment, renovation and first inventory. Neither has owned a business, and they haven't decided how to split ownership or decisions.",
 ["The nature of entrepreneurship and what owners do","Types of business ownership for two partners","The role of finance in starting the business","Creating a start-up and operating budget","Insurance needs for a food business"],
 ["EN:039","BL:003","FI:354","FI:106","FI:571"],
 ["What happens to the business if my friend and I disagree or one of us wants out?","Where can we get the rest of the money we need?"],
 ["Open: 'Three years of custom orders means you already have proof customers want what you make. Now let's build the business around it.'",
  "Entrepreneurship (EN:039): owners take the risk and the reward. You'll lead the product; your partner's management experience covers operations. Divide roles in writing.",
  "Ownership (BL:003): a general partnership is simple but each partner is personally liable for business debts. An LLC protects personal assets and passes profits through to your tax returns. Recommend an LLC with a written operating agreement covering ownership split, decisions, capital and buyouts.",
  "Finance (FI:354): $40,000 contributed against $85,000 needed leaves a $45,000 gap, plus at least 3-6 months of operating reserve. Options: an SBA microloan (up to $50,000), an SBA 7(a) loan through a bank, equipment financing, or a small family investment.",
  "Budget (FI:106): start-up budget (equipment, renovation, permits, deposit, opening inventory) and a monthly operating budget (rent $3,200, ingredients, payroll, utilities, insurance, loan payment). Find break-even: monthly fixed costs / contribution margin per sale.",
  "Insurance (FI:571): general and product liability (food illness claims), commercial property for ovens and equipment, business interruption, workers' compensation once you hire, and a business owner's policy (BOP) to bundle coverage.",
  "Test first: keep the custom-cake business running and add a weekend farmers' market booth to prove storefront demand before signing a long lease.",
  "Visual: a break-even chart and a pie chart of start-up costs.",
  "Judge Q1: Put it in the operating agreement now, while you're friends. Spell out who decides what, how disagreements are settled (for example a mediator), and a buy-sell clause that sets how one partner can buy out the other at a fair, pre-agreed valuation method. Clear rules protect the friendship and the business.",
  "Judge Q2: An SBA microloan can cover up to $50,000, and SBA 7(a) loans through a bank go higher with a government guarantee. Lenders will want a business plan, cash flow projections and your personal investment, which you already have at $40,000. Equipment financing can cover the ovens, using the equipment as collateral."])

case("salon-expansion","Financial Analysis","financial consultants","owner of a hair salon considering a second location",
 "The owner (judge) of a profitable hair salon has steady clients and $210,000 in annual revenue, with net income of $34,000. A second location in a growing neighborhood would cost $120,000 to open and $9,500 a month to run. The owner has $45,000 in business savings and an offer from an investor willing to provide $80,000 in exchange for 30% of the whole business. The owner is excited but nervous about stretching too far.",
 ["The role of finance in an expansion decision","Reading the income statement to judge readiness","Budgeting for the new location","Marginal analysis of the expansion","Managing the risks of growth"],
 ["FI:354","FI:094","FM:013","FI:659","SM:075"],
 ["Should I take the investor's money or a bank loan?","How will I know if the second location is working?"],
 ["Open: 'A profitable salon with loyal clients is the right foundation. The question is how to grow without risking what you've built.'",
  "Income statement (FI:094): net margin is $34,000 / $210,000 = about 16%. Check which services drive profit and whether that margin holds without the owner working every chair.",
  "Finance (FI:354): compare financing. The investor's $80,000 for 30% of the whole business values it at about $267,000, and gives away 30% of current profits forever. A bank or SBA loan keeps 100% ownership, but payments are due every month.",
  "Budget (FM:013): build a 12-month cash budget for location 2: $9,500 monthly costs, a ramp-up of clients over 6 months, loan payments, and a 3-month reserve. Run what-if scenarios at 70%, 100% and 120% of expected bookings.",
  "Marginal analysis (FI:659): compare the extra revenue the second location brings with its extra costs. Break-even = $9,500 / contribution per appointment; with a $40 contribution that's about 238 appointments a month.",
  "Risk (SM:075): risks include slow client build-up, staffing, a lease too long to exit, and spreading the owner too thin. Reduce with a lease that has an exit option, hiring a manager, and pre-booking clients from the current salon's waitlist.",
  "Recommendation: a smaller SBA loan plus $25,000 of savings, keeping full ownership, with a manager hired before opening.",
  "Visual: a break-even chart and a 12-month cash flow line showing the ramp-up.",
  "Judge Q1: For a profitable salon with predictable revenue, a loan is usually better: you keep 100% ownership, interest is tax-deductible, and once it's repaid it's gone. The investor would own 30% of both locations' profits forever. We'd only choose the investor if their expertise or network is worth that stake.",
  "Judge Q2: Set targets before opening: break-even of about 238 appointments a month by month 6, rebooking above 60%, and staff costs under a set share of revenue. We'll review a budget-versus-actual report monthly; if it misses targets for three straight months, we adjust marketing, staffing or hours."])

case("online-shop-cash","Financial Analysis","financial consultants","owner of a growing online clothing shop",
 "The owner (judge) of an online clothing shop doubled sales to $480,000 last year but is constantly short of cash. The shop must pay suppliers 30 days after ordering, while marketplace payouts arrive 14 days after each sale and inventory sits for an average of 75 days. The owner has been covering gaps with a business credit card charging 24% APR and now owes $38,000. The owner tracks finances in a spreadsheet updated once a month.",
 ["Understanding the cash flow statement","The purposes and costs of business credit","Technology tools for accounting","Financial analysis of the shop's performance","How tax rules affect the business's choices"],
 ["FI:091","FI:002","FI:352","FM:014","BL:134"],
 ["How can I be growing so fast and still be out of cash?","Should I get a business line of credit to replace the credit card?"],
 ["Open: 'Growing this fast is a good problem, but cash is the oxygen of a business. Let's fix the flow.'",
  "Cash flow (FI:091): profit isn't cash. Cash goes out when inventory is bought and comes in only when it sells and is paid out. The cash conversion cycle is 75 days of inventory + 14 days to get paid - 30 days to pay suppliers = 59 days the shop is funding its own growth.",
  "Fixes: shorten inventory days (order smaller batches more often, discount slow sellers), negotiate 45-60 day supplier terms, and offer pre-orders on new collections.",
  "Credit (FI:002): credit lets a business bridge timing gaps, but at 24% the card costs about $9,100 a year in interest on $38,000. A bank or SBA line of credit typically costs far less and is designed for working capital.",
  "Technology (FI:352): move from a monthly spreadsheet to cloud accounting (QuickBooks Online or Xero) connected to the bank and marketplace, for daily numbers and a 13-week cash forecast.",
  "Financial analysis (FM:014): track gross margin by product, inventory turnover, and days of cash on hand. Benchmark against similar retailers to find slow products tying up cash.",
  "Taxes (BL:134): interest on business debt is deductible, and with 100% bonus depreciation restored permanently by the 2025 law, qualifying equipment like a new packing system can be deducted in year one. Collect sales tax where the shop meets state thresholds (Wayfair, 2018).",
  "Visual: a cash conversion cycle timeline showing the 59-day gap.",
  "Judge Q1: Because every sale ties up cash for about two months before it comes back. You pay suppliers in 30 days, but inventory sits 75 days and payouts take 14 more. The faster you grow, the more cash gets locked in inventory. Shortening that cycle frees cash without slowing growth.",
  "Judge Q2: Yes. A line of credit is built for exactly this timing gap, usually at a much lower rate than 24%, and you only pay interest on what you draw. Use it to pay off the card, then draw on it only for inventory and repay as sales come in. Set it up now, while sales look strong."])

# ======================= Institution / marketing (2024 format) =======================
case("teen-checking-campaign","Financial Analysis","bank marketing manager and branch manager","president of a community bank",
 "The president (judge) of HARBORVIEW COMMUNITY BANK has noticed that local high school students are opening accounts with app-only fintech companies instead of the bank. The president wants your team to design a one-page brochure and a short in-school presentation promoting the bank's new teen checking and savings accounts, which have no monthly fees, a debit card, parental alerts and a savings account paying 4.00% APY on balances up to $1,000.",
 ["Forms of financial exchange teens will use","The role of financial institutions and deposit insurance","Managing online accounts safely","Why teens should save early","Writing a persuasive message for teens"],
 ["FI:058","FI:336","FI:830","FI:270","CO:031"],
 ["Why should a teen choose our bank instead of an app like Cash App?","What will make students actually read the brochure?"],
 ["Open: 'We'll show students that a bank account can be as easy as an app, and safer.'",
  "Forms of exchange (FI:058): cash, debit cards, mobile wallets, peer-to-peer payments, checks and direct deposit. Cash is now only about 1 in 7 consumer payments (Federal Reserve), so teens need digital tools.",
  "Role of financial institutions (FI:336): banks keep money safe, make payments and lend. FDIC insurance covers $250,000 per depositor, per bank. Some app balances depend on partner banks; the 2024 Synapse collapse locked app customers out of their money for months.",
  "Online safety (FI:830): two-factor login, instant alerts, card lock in the app, and Regulation E: report an unauthorized debit within 2 business days and your loss is capped at $50.",
  "Save early (FI:270): $1,000 at 4.00% earns $40 a year with no risk; saving $20 a week from 16 to 18 builds about $2,000 plus interest, a head start on a car or college.",
  "Persuasive brochure (CO:031): lead with benefits, not features: 'Get paid faster with direct deposit,' 'Lock your card in one tap,' 'Your money is FDIC-insured.' Short headline, three bullet benefits, a QR code to open an account and a clear call to action.",
  "Presentation: 10 minutes at school, a quick quiz with a prize, real teen scenarios (first paycheck, splitting pizza money), and a parent sign-up night.",
  "Measure success: number of teen accounts opened, direct deposits set up and average savings balance after 6 months.",
  "Judge Q1: Our accounts match the app experience (mobile deposit, instant payments, card controls) and add things apps often don't: FDIC insurance directly at our bank, a 4.00% savings rate, local branches and people who can help. And if an app's partner fails, as Synapse did in 2024, customers can lose access to their money.",
  "Judge Q2: Make it about them, not the bank: a bold headline like 'Your first paycheck, upgraded,' three short benefits, real numbers, bright design and a QR code to sign up in minutes. Test a draft with a few students first and use their words."])

case("cu-retirement-seminar","Financial Analysis","credit union wealth managers","CEO of a credit union",
 "The CEO (judge) of RIVERBEND CREDIT UNION is worried that many members in their 30s and 40s have little or no retirement savings. The CEO wants your team to plan and present a free evening retirement seminar for members, explaining retirement accounts, Social Security and investing basics, and encouraging members to meet with the credit union's advisors.",
 ["Retirement planning and account types","Calculating the time value of money","Types of investments for retirement","The responsibilities of finance professionals to members","Delivering an effective oral presentation"],
 ["FI:569","FI:238","FI:077","CR:012","CO:025"],
 ["What should a 45-year-old with no savings do first?","How do we make sure members trust our advisors and don't feel sold to?"],
 ["Open: 'One evening can change members' retirements. We'll make it simple, honest and action-focused.'",
  "Retirement accounts (FI:569): 401(k)/403(b) with employer match (limit $24,500 in 2026, $25,500 in 2027; catch-up from 50), IRAs and Roth IRAs ($7,500 in 2026 and 2027), and starting in 2027 the Saver's Match of up to $1,000 for eligible savers. Social Security replaces only about 40% of average income.",
  "Time value of money (FI:238): $300 a month at 7% for 30 years grows to about $366,000; the same $300 for 20 years grows to about $156,000. Waiting 10 years costs more than half. Show it on one slide.",
  "Investments (FI:077): stocks, bonds, index funds and target-date funds; low fees (index funds often under 0.10%) leave more money compounding.",
  "Responsibilities (CR:012): put members' interests first, disclose how advisors are paid, explain options in plain language, keep information confidential.",
  "Presentation (CO:025): open with a story or striking number, 3 key points, one simple chart per point, live Q&A, and a clear next step (sign up for a free 30-minute review that night).",
  "Promotion: email, app notification, posters in branches, and partner with local employers.",
  "Measure: attendance, review appointments booked, and new IRA and payroll-deduction accounts within 90 days.",
  "Judge Q1: Start with the employer match, since it's an instant return, and open a Roth or traditional IRA. Catch-up contributions start at 50, so the next five years matter. Even $400 a month for 20 years at 7% grows to about $208,000, and delaying Social Security toward 70 raises guaranteed income.",
  "Judge Q2: Be transparent and educational. Explain exactly how advisors are paid, give useful information members can act on even if they never book a meeting, and invite, don't pressure. As a not-for-profit credit union, the mission is the members, which is a message to lead with."])

case("robo-advisor-launch","Financial-Information Management","fintech consultants","CEO of a regional brokerage firm",
 "The CEO (judge) of SUMMITPOINT INVESTMENTS wants to launch a low-cost robo-advisor app to attract younger investors, who currently use big online brokerages. The app would build and rebalance portfolios automatically and use AI chat to answer customer questions. The board is excited but worried about compliance, cybersecurity and protecting customer data.",
 ["Use of technology in financial-information management","Use of technology in compliance","Using CRM technology to serve investors","Use of technology in risk management","Legal considerations in the finance industry"],
 ["FM:011","BL:149","CR:024","RM:042","BL:133"],
 ["What if the AI chat gives a customer bad advice?","How do we compete with huge brokerages that already offer robo-advisors?"],
 ["Open: 'Younger investors want low cost, simple and mobile. A robo-advisor can win them, if trust is built in from day one.'",
  "Technology (FM:011): cloud platform with real-time data, automated portfolio building and rebalancing, ERP integration for accounting, encryption and firewalls.",
  "Compliance technology (BL:149): automated KYC (ID plus selfie verification), sanctions screening, archiving every customer message, and suitability checks built into onboarding.",
  "CRM (CR:024): track each customer's goals and life events, send personalized nudges (raise contributions after a raise), and measure customer lifetime value.",
  "Risk technology (RM:042): intrusion detection, multifactor authentication, regular backups, and AI fraud monitoring. Test AI models for accuracy and bias, and keep humans reviewing.",
  "Legal (BL:133): the firm is regulated by the SEC and FINRA; recommendations must meet Regulation Best Interest; provide Form CRS; customer accounts are protected by SIPC up to $500,000 if the firm fails (not against market losses).",
  "Pricing: robo-advisors commonly charge around 0.25% a year or less; low cost is the main draw.",
  "Visual: an onboarding flowchart from sign-up to first automated investment, with compliance checkpoints marked.",
  "Judge Q1: Limit the AI to education and account help, not personal recommendations, with clear disclosures, and route anything about specific investment advice to a licensed human. Log every conversation for compliance, test the model regularly, and have a fast correction process. Regulators hold the firm responsible for what its tools say.",
  "Judge Q2: Compete on what big firms can't easily match: local human advisors on call, personalized goal coaching through the CRM, and a lower minimum. Partner with local employers and credit unions to reach young workers, and make trust and transparency the brand."])

case("bank-merger-customers","Customer Relations","customer experience managers","president of a community bank being acquired",
 "The president (judge) of MAPLE VALLEY BANK, a 70-year-old community bank, has agreed to be acquired by a larger regional bank. Account numbers, the mobile app and some fees will change in four months, and two of the bank's six branches will close. Longtime customers are already calling, worried about losing personal service. The president wants your team's plan for communicating the merger and keeping customers.",
 ["The nature of convergence and consolidation in banking","The role of financial institutions in the community","Interpreting new policies for customers","Handling customer complaints","Building trust during the transition"],
 ["FI:573","FI:336","CR:007","CR:010","EI:128"],
 ["Why should customers stay instead of moving to another local bank?","How should employees respond to a customer who is angry about the branch closing?"],
 ["Open: 'Mergers succeed or fail on how customers feel. We'll make sure nobody is surprised.'",
  "Consolidation (FI:573): the number of FDIC-insured institutions fell to 4,238 by mid-2026, from more than 17,000 in 1984. Mergers give scale: bigger ATM networks, better apps and more products. Recent examples include Capital One's 2025 acquisition of Discover.",
  "Role of institutions (FI:336): the bank keeps deposits safe (FDIC insurance continues; during the transition, deposits at the two banks are insured separately for six months), lends locally and serves the community. Emphasize that local lending continues.",
  "Interpret policies (CR:007): a plain-language guide: what changes, what doesn't, the dates, and what customers need to do (often nothing). Explain why each change happens and what's better.",
  "Complaints (CR:010): Listen, Apologize, Solve, Thank. Train every employee with answers to the top 10 questions and authority to waive a fee for upset customers.",
  "Trust (EI:128): personal calls to the top 500 relationships, the same local bankers staying, a dedicated transition hotline, and branch 'welcome days' to learn the new app.",
  "Timeline: letters and emails 90, 60 and 30 days out; app training sessions; extra staff in the first two weeks after conversion.",
  "Measure: customer retention rate, complaint volume, app adoption and survey scores.",
  "Judge Q1: Because the people they trust are staying, and now they'll have more: a larger ATM network, a better app and more loan options, with their deposits still FDIC-insured. We'll show exactly what improves for them and make the switch effortless.",
  "Judge Q2: Listen without interrupting, acknowledge that the closing is a real inconvenience, and apologize for the disruption. Then solve: show the nearest branch and ATM, offer help setting up mobile deposit, and give a direct number for their banker. Thank them for being a customer and follow up in a week."])

